;; The appliance's one job: encode a video source and send it to one peer.
;;
;; It carries three files and states no symbols of its own. The pipeline is a
;; systemd unit, its destination is /etc/default/camera-stream, and the unit
;; is enabled by a symlink in the overlay — because installing a unit and
;; enabling it are two different things, which a k3s image learned the hard
;; way by booting with its service never started.
;;
;; There is no transport here. The unit sends to whatever address the config
;; names and the kernel routes it: a LAN address on the bench, a WireGuard
;; address in the field. Compose feature:wireguard alongside and neither
;; fragment needs to know about the other.

(fragment feature:camera-stream

  (doc "H.264 over RTP/UDP to one peer, as a systemd service")

  ;; It cannot require feature:gstreamer: only targets and profiles provide
  ;; capabilities, because a feature providing one would make composition
  ;; order-dependent. So the image names both features, and this says which
  ;; elements the unit expects to find.
  (requires
    (capability mmu)
    (capability systemd-init))

  (buildroot
    ;; The unit, its defaults, and the symlink that enables it. (path ...)
    ;; checks the directory exists before anything builds, emits it as
    ;; $(BR2_EXTERNAL_CAMERA_STREAM_PATH)/files for Buildroot to expand, and
    ;; hashes its contents into the solution — a changed pipeline is a
    ;; different appliance.
    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "files")))
